Input: {"question": "What was the rank of General Seitz?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d00", "start": 0, "end": 7, "text": "John A."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d00", "start": 8, "end": 62, "text": "Seitz Brigadier General John \"Andy\" Andrew Seitz (est."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d00", "start": 63, "end": 172, "text": "1908 – January 4, 1987) was the commanding general of the 1st Infantry Division and the XVIII Airborne Corps."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d00", "start": 173, "end": 262, "text": "General Seitz was born in Leavenworth, Kansas and graduated from Leavenworth High School."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d00", "start": 263, "end": 308, "text": "His brother was Lieutenant General Richard J."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d00", "start": 309, "end": 315, "text": "Seitz."}, "D1S7": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d00", "start": 316, "end": 370, "text": "Seitz was a 1932 graduate of the University of Kansas."}, "D1S8": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d00", "start": 371, "end": 447, "text": "In 1987, he was a recipient of the school's Mildred Clodfelter Alumni Award."}, "D1S9": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d00", "start": 448, "end": 465, "text": "He had two wives."}, "D1S10": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d00", "start": 466, "end": 568, "text": "His second wife was Dorothy \"Dottie\" Seitz (1918-2010), who was previously married to Colonel Seaborn."}, "D1S11": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d00", "start": 569, "end": 611, "text": "Seitz had a son, Colonel John Andrew Seitz"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d01", "start": 0, "end": 107, "text": "newspapers · books · scholar · JSTOR ( February 2020 ) ( Learn how and when to remove this message ) Brig ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d01", "start": 108, "end": 112, "text": "Gen."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d01", "start": 113, "end": 120, "text": "John A."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d01", "start": 121, "end": 559, "text": "Seitz Died Junction City , Kansas Allegiance United States of America Service / branch United States Army Rank Brigadier General Commands 1st Infantry Division XVIII Airborne Corps Awards Distinguished Service Medal Legion of Merit with Oak Leaf Cluster Master Parachutist Badge Brigadier General John Andrew Seitz ( 1908 – January 4 , 1987 ) was the American commanding general of the 1st Infantry Division and the XVIII Airborne Corps ."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d01", "start": 560, "end": 572, "text": "[ 1 ] Family"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d02", "start": 0, "end": 24, "text": "Ridgefield, Connecticut."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d02", "start": 25, "end": 72, "text": "[3] Early in 1972, Royce also died from cancer."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d02", "start": 73, "end": 185, "text": "[3] Later that year, Seitz suffered a stroke after which he required nursing home care for the rest of his life."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d02", "start": 186, "end": 216, "text": "[3] Lieutenant Colonel John F."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d02", "start": 217, "end": 219, "text": "R."}, "D3S6": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d02", "start": 220, "end": 257, "text": "\"Jeff\" Seitz died in Washington, D.C."}, "D3S7": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d02", "start": 258, "end": 278, "text": "on October 10, 1978."}, "D3S8": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d02", "start": 279, "end": 327, "text": "[2] He is buried in Arlington National Cemetery."}, "D3S9": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d02", "start": 328, "end": 395, "text": "[2] In his memorial article for Seitz, Brigadier General William D."}, "D3S10": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d02", "start": 396, "end": 527, "text": "Thompson wrote that \"as an officer he had outstanding\" leadership qualities and was deeply respected by his peers and subordinates."}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d03", "start": 0, "end": 10, "text": "Richard J."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d03", "start": 11, "end": 311, "text": "Seitz Lieutenant General Richard \"Dick\" Joe Seitz (February 18, 1918 – June 8, 2013) was an American Army officer and paratrooper who, during a 35-year career, commanded the 2nd Battalion, 517th Parachute Infantry Regiment during World War II, the 82nd Airborne Division and the XVIII Airborne Corps."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d03", "start": 312, "end": 360, "text": "Seitz and his brother, Brigadier General John A."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d03", "start": 361, "end": 442, "text": "\"Andy\" Seitz, both graduated from Leavenworth High School in Leavenworth, Kansas."}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d03", "start": 443, "end": 534, "text": "Seitz graduated from high school in 1937 and was cadet commander of his school's ROTC unit."}, "D4S6": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d03", "start": 535, "end": 617, "text": "He also received the American Legion Cup as the outstanding cadet his senior year."}, "D4S7": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d03", "start": 618, "end": 632, "text": "In Leavenworth"}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d04", "start": 0, "end": 203, "text": "] On December 11 , 1944 , Major General Huebner became deputy commander of V Corps and soon thereafter became commander when Lieutenant General Gerow became commander of the new 15th United States Army ."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d04", "start": 204, "end": 290, "text": "[ 134 ] Brigadier General Clift Andrus became commander of the 1st Infantry Division ."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d04", "start": 291, "end": 537, "text": "[ 134 ] Battle of the Bulge : Bütgenbach [ edit ] After six months of almost constant campaigning and combat , the 1st Infantry Division units had been off the front line near Aubel , Belgium for only a few days when on December 16 , 1944 , three"}, "D6S1": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d05", "start": 0, "end": 71, "text": "war – depended on how well that private fought in the next hour or so ."}, "D6S2": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d05", "start": 72, "end": 163, "text": "'' [ 3 ] Footnotes [ edit ] ^ More than 317,000 German soldiers surrendered to the 1st U.S."}, "D6S3": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d05", "start": 164, "end": 227, "text": "Army in the Falaise pocket in late March and early April 1945 ."}, "D6S4": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d05", "start": 228, "end": 249, "text": "Wheeler , 2007 , pp ."}, "D6S5": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d05", "start": 250, "end": 258, "text": "379–80 ."}, "D6S6": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d05", "start": 259, "end": 331, "text": "^ The nickname was passed along to a son and grandson of the same name ."}, "D6S7": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d05", "start": 332, "end": 363, "text": "Thompson , Assembly , 1979 , p."}, "D6S8": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d05", "start": 364, "end": 369, "text": "137 ."}, "D6S9": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d05", "start": 370, "end": 400, "text": "^ Lieutenant General George S."}, "D6S10": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d05", "start": 401, "end": 483, "text": "Patton , commander of the 7th United States Army , specifically requested that the"}, "D7S1": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d06", "start": 0, "end": 34, "text": "by grandchildren) in Tehran, Iran."}, "D7S2": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d06", "start": 35, "end": 154, "text": "After Seitz's retirement in 1966, he and Royce had an apartment in New York City and a home in Ridgefield, Connecticut."}, "D7S3": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d06", "start": 155, "end": 198, "text": "Early in 1972, Royce also died from cancer."}, "D7S4": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d06", "start": 199, "end": 307, "text": "Later that year, Seitz suffered a stroke after which he required nursing home care for the rest of his life."}, "D7S5": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d06", "start": 308, "end": 329, "text": "Major General John F."}, "D7S6": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d06", "start": 330, "end": 332, "text": "R."}, "D7S7": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d06", "start": 333, "end": 370, "text": "\"Jeff\" Seitz died in Washington, D.C."}, "D7S8": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d06", "start": 371, "end": 391, "text": "on October 10, 1978."}, "D7S9": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d06", "start": 392, "end": 436, "text": "He is buried in Arlington National Cemetery."}, "D7S10": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d06", "start": 437, "end": 500, "text": "In his memorial article for Seitz, Brigadier General William D."}, "D7S11": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d06", "start": 501, "end": 581, "text": "Thompson wrote that \"as an officer he had outstanding ability; he was a superior"}, "D8S1": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d07", "start": 0, "end": 203, "text": "] On December 11 , 1944 , Major General Huebner became deputy commander of V Corps and soon thereafter became commander when Lieutenant General Gerow became commander of the new 15th United States Army ."}, "D8S2": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d07", "start": 204, "end": 290, "text": "[ 134 ] Brigadier General Clift Andrus became commander of the 1st Infantry Division ."}, "D8S3": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d07", "start": 291, "end": 537, "text": "[ 134 ] Battle of the Bulge : Bütgenbach [ edit ] After six months of almost constant campaigning and combat , the 1st Infantry Division units had been off the front line near Aubel , Belgium for only a few days when on December 16 , 1944 , three"}, "D9S1": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d08", "start": 0, "end": 102, "text": "the film for 35mm and home-video release , it had to use a dupe negative as the basis for their work ."}, "D9S2": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d08", "start": 103, "end": 278, "text": "“ The dupe had a lot of printed-in defects , including dirt and scratches , ” says Phil Murphy , Paramount ’ s senior vice president of operations in the television division ."}, "D9S3": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a:d08", "start": 279, "end": 323, "text": "“ Certain sections had been poorly exposed ."}, "D9S4": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0491-00